[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s Linux firewall (iptables) on the inter-site/transit interface. Routing (FRR/RIP) is healthy, but the forwarding policy is wrong.

Mechanism: Traffic from Location A (172.16.10.0/24) destined to Location C (172.18.50/51.0/24) reaches router6 via the transit link 10.50.1.0/24 (router6 eth2). To reach the LANs, router6 must forward from eth2 to eth0/eth1. However, router6’s FORWARD chain drops all packets arriving on eth2 (except a single narrow TCP:80 exception), so inter-site ICMP and most traffic are discarded at router6. Locally generated traffic from router6 to Location C hosts succeeds (not subject to FORWARD), which matches our probes.

Localization: 
- From pc4/pc5 and upstream routers (router0/1/2/3) to pc15 (172.18.51.15): timeouts.
- From router6 to pc15: OK (sub-millisecond).
- Router3 to router6’s own interface (172.18.50.254): OK.
- Router6 iptables shows:
  -P FORWARD ACCEPT
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
  -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
These rules categorically block forwarding of transit traffic.

Root cause: Misconfigured iptables on router6 that drops all forwarded traffic received on eth2 (10.50.1.2), likely re-applied after a reboot or “router thing” service action.

Corrective fix: On router6, remove/adjust the FORWARD rules to allow forwarding from eth2 to LANs (e.g., iptables -F FORWARD; or allow -m state ESTABLISHED,RELATED and permit eth2→eth0/eth1). Ensure the persistent firewall config is corrected so the DROP rules on -i eth2 are not reloaded. After this, inter-site reachability from Location A to Location C will be restored. 
=== END ===